\f0\b\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf2 \strokec2 Questions
\f2\fs48 \cf0 \strokec3 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f3\b0\fs32 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}1. \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 What is an RDD? Explain the meaning of "Resilient," "Distributed," and "Dataset" in Resilient Distributed Dataset.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 An RDD is basically a big list of data that Spark splits into pieces and hands out to many computers to work on at the same time.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Resilient means it can bounce back from problems. If one computer crashes and its piece of data is lost, Spark rebuilds just that piece instead of starting over.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Distributed means the data is spread across many computers, not sitting on just one.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Dataset just means a collection of data, like lines in a file or a list of numbers.
\f1 \cf0 \cb1 \
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf0 \strokec3 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}2. \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Explain the difference between transformations and actions in RDDs. Give two examples of each.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 	
\f3\b0\fs24 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \'95	\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 A transformation is an instruction like \'93keep only the even numbers\'94 or \'93double every number.\'94 Spark writes it down but doesn\'92t do it yet. Examples: 
\fs26 map
\fs24  and 
\fs26 filter
\fs24 .\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 An action is when you finally say \'93OK, show me the answer.\'94 That\'92s when Spark does the work. Examples: 
\fs26 count
\fs24  and 
\fs26 collect
\fs24 .\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec3 Think of a recipe. Transformations are the steps written on the page. The action is actually turning on the stove.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs32 \cf0 \strokec3 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f3\b0 \cf0 {\listtext	1	}3. What is lazy evaluation in Spark? Why does Spark use it, and how does benefit RDD processing?\
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs24 {\listtext	2	}Spark is LAZY on purpose. It doesn\'92t do anything until you ask for a final result.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf0      - it can plan ahead. Like a delivery driver who\'92s less all the stops first and pick the best route, Spark combines your step into efficient pass.\
    - it saves effort. If you only ask for the first 5 results, it doesn\'92t process the whole dataset.\
    - it doesn\'92t waste memory storing every in between result.
\fs32 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f3\b0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}4. \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 What is RDD lineage (DAG)? How does it help Spark achieve fault tolerance without replicating data?\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 {\listtext	2	}Lineage Spark memory of how your data was made, like a recipe card. Start with this file, filter it, then map it. The diagram of those steps is called a DAG.\
{\listtext	3	}If a computer fails and loses a piece of data, spare just looks at the recipe and re cook only the missing piece. Many system kept several backup copies of everything, which takes lots of space. Spark only saves the receipt, which is tiny.\
{\listtext	4	}The catch: if the recipe is very long, re cooking can take a while. You can save a checkpoint partway through to avoid that.
\f0\b\fs32 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f3\b0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}5. \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Differentiate between narrow and wide transformations. Why do wide transformations cause a shuffle?\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf0    - 
\fs24 Narrow: each worker only needs their own data. Example. Double every number, nobody need to talk to anybody. Fast\
   - wide: worker need data from other workers. Example add up all the sales per product. The sales for one product might be scattered across many computer.
\fs32 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f3\b0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}6. \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Which is better , Python or Scala while working with Spark.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Both are good and have their particularity. Python(pyspark) is easier to learn, has tons of tutorials and tolls, and iris very common in jobs. Scala is a bit faster in some cases and gets new spark features first, but its harder to learn. Of you are using DataFrames, the speed difference is small. For most people starting out, Python is the better choice
\fs32 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f3\b0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}7. \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 What is the difference between SparkSession and SparkContect\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 SparkContext- is the old way to connect your program to spark\
SparkSession_ is the newer, all in one way. It does everything SparkContext does and more.
\fs32 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\f3\b0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}8. \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 What is the difference between RDD and Dataframes\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf0 RDD- 
\fs24 raw data with no structure, like a pile of loose papers. You have lots of control, but Spark can\'92t optimize\
DataFrame- data organized in row and column with names, like an excel table. Spark understand it, so it run it faster and the code is shorter.\
For most work, use DataFrames. Uses RDD only when you need very fine control or have messy, unstructured data.
\fs32 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f3\b0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}9. \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 What is driver program ? Spark driver\
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 The Driver is the boss of your spark program. It:\
-Read your code\
-break the job into small test\
-hand those task to the workers(called executors)\
-track progress and collect the final results\
\
One warning: if you ask the driver to collect everything (with collect()) from a huge dataset, it can run out of memory, like asking the boss to hold every worker paperwork at once. 
\fs32 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0
\f3\b0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}10. \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 Where does Spark stores the data?\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Spark splits big data into pieces(RDD), gives them to many workers, waits until you ask for an answer, plans the fastest way to do ir and can rebuild any lost piece from its receipe. 
\fs32 \
